hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in his essay on truth and lying in an  extra moral sense  Nietzsche begins by talking about the  human intellect and this is a faculty  that throughout the history of  philosophy up until Nietzsche's time and  and really even still today in in most  philosophical traditions he has been  understood as not only being the highest  faculty in human beings but as our means  of access to modes of knowledge or even  of life that are both typically human in  a certain way but also go beyond the  human the intellect is understood is  sort of opening up the universe to us in  a way that's not possible for non  rational animals animals that lack and  intellect or these sorts of faculties  and Nietzsche begins by in effect  deflating the intellect he's got this  interesting you know story here that  he's telling he says in some remote  corner of the universe that is poured  out in countless flickering solar  systems there was once a star on which  clever animals invented knowledge this  that was the most arrogant in the most  untruthful moment in world history yet  indeed only a moment after nature taken  a few breaths the star froze over and  the clever animals he had to die and  then he goes in and he says someone  could invent such a fable this is a  fable for him and still not have  Illustrated adequately how pitiful how  shadowy and fleeting how purposeless and  arbitrary the human intellect appears in  nature so there's the story that the  intellect tells itself and that we tell  ourselves about the intellect and then  there's the reality of the intellect  within nature and notice what what  Nietzsche says there's really two sort  of lines of complaint or a denigration  going on here one is about it's its own  lack of capacities it's shadowy right it  is it is not what it appears to be and  the other has to do with purpose with  having a function a real function and  and Nietzsche does think the intellect  has a function but it's not what we  pretend that it is it's not the story  again that we we tell ourselves so he  goes on and he says if we want to  compare ourselves to other kinds of  being you know the intellect has no  further mission leading beyond human  life he says it's utterly human only its  owner and producer takes it with such  pathos as if the whole world hinged upon  it if we could communicate with the mat  or any other animal indeed it would say  that its faculties are just as important  and its view of the world is just as  central he talks about it feels itself  the flying center of the world so this  means that the intellect is really in a  kind of self delusion about its own  importance and about the importance of  the animal the human being that bears  that Faculty so what is the function of  the intellect I just mentioned that  Nietzsche thinks that its purpose is  ultimately to serve human life not to go  beyond the realm of life but just to  conduce to two things we we are the  animals that have developed in such a  way as we have this this this skill or  this faculty or this capacity that the  others lack for thinking things out or  they don't they don't have it developed  to the same extent for taking the world  and representing it inside of our heads  and tinkering around with it and even  applying it to our own selves thinking  about why we do the things we do why we  say the things we say why we feel the  way that we do we develop that according  to Nietzsche not so that we can grasp  truth itself to begin with for Nietzsche  truth itself is kind of a figment of our  imagination and our language but he says  it quite clearly here that the  intellects role is not primarily  apprehending truth the intellects role  is to serve life to  help us live so he talks about it here  he says in reality this faculty is given  only as help to the most unfortunate  most delicate and most perishable  creature and if you think about how  that's the case you know we are the  creature that has the big old brain that  requires that babies have to be brought  up and they're helpless for a much much  longer time the baby has to eventually  develop intellectually right and and we  lack many of the instincts that other  animals do that just sort of kick in and  get them where they need to go and have  them doing what they need to do we have  to kind of figure it all out and we  often go astray with that sort of thing  all right we're plasticity of our nature  can be a boon or an encumbrance so  there's that end of it but then even as  adults what is our intellect therefore  it's to help us this vulnerable creature  I mean look at us right we have these  nails that aren't really claws we have  these tiny little teeth you know of  course our bite is pretty toxic because  of all the bacteria that we carry in our  mouths but we can't really rely on that  we're able to do things because we apply  our intellects and that is the advantage  that we have over other creatures so he  goes on he says the intellect is a means  of preserving the individual a means of  preserving one's life of maintaining the  integrity of one's body you could say  and then he goes on and says it develops  its main powers in dis simulation and  German fierce tell'em setting something  else out in place of what really is  substituting one thing for another and  if you think about the way in which the  intellect does work that is an important  part of it you know we talk about  processes for example of abstraction  what does abstraction really it is  depending on you know what psychological  theory you have for it it's taking one  thing and having it stand in  a whole bunch of others and this is  something he talks about quite a bit in  terms of concepts and language a little  bit later on in the essay but I did want  to talk about the modes of dis  simulation that he talks about here and  we're gonna look both at the English and  the German for this just in part because  it's nice to to compare the two so he  says this art of dis simulation right  which we use as a tool both to get by in  relation to the other animals in our  environment and other people and also to  to help ourselves psychologically deal  with with life he says this art of  dissimulation reaches its peak in man  here deception flattery lying and  cheating slander false pretenses living  on borrowed glory masquerading  conventions of concealment play-acting  before others and before oneself in some  the constant fluttering about the flame  of vanity is so much the rule and the  law that almost nothing is more  incomprehensible than how an honest and  pure desire for truth could arise among  men how is it that if we look at human  beings and we see all these different  ways in which they fool other people and  try to fool the the other animals in the  world and fool themselves how did the  desire for truth arise so that that's an  interesting question  that's not one that we're gonna worry  over much about in this particular  discussion so you know he says what what  are these in in in German right he says  Tyshawn right deception flattery Schmidt  Schmidt :  right lugan and true again nice you know  thing right they're lying and cheating  then when he talks about slander that is  literally the behind the back talking  hinter Dirac and Radin represent earrin  is this false pretences dasya manborg  and a glance  Leoben dust mask mask ayrton mascara  design sorry masquerading you know  placing a mask on oneself taking on a  persona the original meaning of persona  was in fact mask right that that an  actor would put on by the way hypocrite  comes from the word for actor you know  the one who was literally before the  judges he goes out and he talks about  conventions of concealment right  conventions of concealment whoo-hoo  Linda convention conventions of hiding  things conventions for hiding things we  live by a number of conventions that  tell us how to get by in the world but  also hide the true nature's of things  from us or at least you know if we don't  want to talk about truth just what the  things are he goes on and he says  play-acting before others and oneself  right Boonen schpeel for andhra and  fours except right so this is all ways  of using the intellect using the  capacities that we have to substitute  one thing in place of another in order  to gain not truth but some sort of  advantage for life and the better the  intellect is the more facile it is in  discovering the means of persuasion and  confusion and concealment and  dissimilation so what else do we need to  say about the intellect and its role  here in this essay on truth and lying in  an extra moral sense the last thing that  we see him talking about throughout this  is that the intellect really does seem  to grasp and form concepts and even laws  of nature but Nietzsche is suspicious of  this he thinks that this is really a  kind of lying right conventionalized  sort of lying that the intellect itself  engages in so these these very laws of  nature or concepts are  themselves forms of dis simulation and  that is what the intellect does best
 

